WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:09.390
This video explains how to use the Arclight to perform the fundal red reflex by selecting the brightest light setting.

2
00:00:09.730 --> 00:00:16.650
Make sure the lens rack is at the top, where there is no lens power in the sight hole.

3
00:00:16.970 --> 00:00:22.010
At arm's length, observe the reflex in both eyes at the same time.

4
00:00:22.530 --> 00:00:26.750
Compare the brightness and colour between the two eyes.

5
00:00:26.750 --> 00:00:35.290
In a normal examination, there should be no overall difference in the brightness and colour between the two eyes.

6
00:00:36.340 --> 00:00:42.120
In white and Asian babies, the reflex can look more orange and yellow.

7
00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:50.500
These appearances are still all normal as the overall colour and brightness are the same between the two eyes.

8
00:00:50.500 --> 00:00:58.980
An abnormal reflex is when there is a persistent, real difference in the brightness and colour between the two eyes.

9
00:00:59.380 --> 00:01:10.890
This may be a displaced corneal reflection, a dark patch present within the reflex of one eye or both eyes, or a completely absent red reflex.

10
00:01:12.570 --> 00:01:25.670
An abnormal reflex test can indicate the presence of cataract, retinoblastoma, corneal scar, squint, or refractive error.

11
00:01:26.110 --> 00:01:29.970
These all require further assessment by a specialist.

